This rule covers two things: (1) correct phrasing of "one and a half" with nouns, and (2) singular vs. plural verb agreement with time/quantity expressions.

Part 1: Two Correct Ways to Say "1.5"
Structure	Noun form	Example
One and a half + noun	Plural	one and a half hours
A/An + noun + and a half	Singular	an hour and a half

Both mean the same thing (1.5 hours), but the noun form flips depending on word order:

✅ One and a half hours (plural noun, because "one and a half" as a whole = more than one)
✅ An hour and a half (singular noun first, since it starts with "an" — one hour + half more)
❌ One and a half hour (wrong — can't use singular noun with "one and a half")
❌ Hours and a half (wrong — can't use plural noun right after "a/an")

Same pattern applies to any unit:

Two and a half kilometers ✅ / Two kilometers and a half ✅
One and a half kilograms ✅ / A kilogram and a half ✅


Part 2: Singular Verb, Even Though It's "More Than One"

One and a half years was wasted. ✅ (not "were")
A year and a half was wasted. ✅ (not "were")

Why singular verb ("was"), even with plural-sounding nouns like "years"?

This follows a broader rule: expressions of time, money, distance, or weight, when thought of as ONE complete unit/quantity, take a singular verb — even if the noun itself is grammatically plural.

Think of "one and a half years" as one single stretch of time (a duration), not as multiple separate "years" — so it's treated as one unit → singular verb.

Compare with similar time/quantity rules you may already know:

Ten years is a long time. (one period, singular verb)
Five thousand rupees was stolen. (one amount, singular verb)
Two miles is too far to walk. (one distance, singular verb)



One and a half years was wasted. ✅
→ The entire period is viewed as one block of time.
One and a half years were spent on different projects. ✅
→ The years are viewed as separate units.

This is the same principle as:

Ten years is a long time. ✅
Ten years were divided into two periods. ✅


Rule to remember:

A number + plural unit → usually plural verb, BUT if the expression represents one single amount/period → singular verb.
